# Shipwright: Genzo, 44. Gruff, dependable, short sentences, soft underneath.
# Built half the boats in the bay and fixed the other half.
# Bet lost: his hands. Koban called his best boat, the Ōkujira, a bathtub. He bet
# his hands it could outsail anything she owned; she said they'd roll for it.
# She rolled a six, he rolled a five. His hands have worked for the House since,
# carving dice without him. Freed, they're his again, but stiff and shaky.
# Koban's seal: gold coin marks on the backs of both hands.
# Perk: Brace (start every turn with block). Second to be rescued.
# Speaker id: shipwright. Faces: neutral, happy, tease, flustered, sad, surprised.

~ rescue_start

The cage here is a ship's cradle, ribs of black wood with a broad man sitting inside on an upturned bucket. His hands are carving dice, fast and neat, and dropping them on a pile at his feet. He doesn't look at them. [#face=neutral]
shipwright: Don't mind the hands. They're on the House's clock. [#face=neutral]
shipwright: Three locks on this cage. I built two of them. Sorry about that. [#face=tease]
- You built your own cage?
	shipwright: The hands did. I watched. Good joints, too. That's the worst of it. [#face=tease]
- Any advice?
	shipwright: The pair lock sticks. Give it both dice at once and don't be gentle. [#face=neutral]
shipwright: Water's rising. Get on with it. Please. [#face=neutral]
=> END

~ rescue_turn

% shipwright: Steady. Rushing sinks more boats than storms do. [#face=neutral]
% shipwright: That one was close. Keep at it. [#face=neutral]
% shipwright: My boots are wet. That's fine. I've had wet boots for twenty years. [#face=happy]
=> END

~ rescue_won

The third lock gives. The cradle creaks open, and the carving stops all at once. He stares at his hands as if they belong to someone else. [#face=surprised]
shipwright: They stopped. [#face=surprised]
shipwright: Genzo. Shipwright. I built half the boats in this bay, and the other half I fixed. [#face=neutral]
- What did you bet?
	shipwright: My hands. You saw. [#face=neutral]
- Why were your hands carving dice?
	shipwright: Because they're not mine. Weren't. [#face=neutral]
shipwright: The cat came up to look at the Ōkujira, my best boat, and called it a bathtub. [#face=sad]
shipwright: I said I'd bet my hands it could outsail anything she owned. She said fine, but we roll for it. [#face=sad]
shipwright: She rolled a six. I rolled a five. Since then my hands have worked for the House, and I've worked for nobody. [#face=sad]
He turns his hands over. On the back of each one sits a small gold mark, round, with a square hole, like a coin pressed into the skin.
shipwright: They're stiff. Might be a while before I can build anything worth sailing. [#face=sad]
shipwright: Until then I can still stand in front of you. When the hits come, I'll take the first of them. [#face=neutral]
=> END

~ rescue_failed

The tide covers the last lock. The cradle groans shut and sinks back into the dark, and you hear the carving start again. [#face=neutral]
shipwright: Not your fault. These locks are well made. I'd know. [#face=happy]
shipwright: Next tide. I'll be here. Hands'll keep busy, so take your time. [#face=neutral]
=> END

~ move_in

He walks the length of the cottage, knocks on a post, and listens to it like a doctor. [#face=neutral]
shipwright: Sound frame. Somebody built this right. [#face=neutral]
if villager_happy
	shipwright: And I can smell the tar from the shed. That'll do. That'll do fine. [#face=happy]
else
	shipwright: Bit far from the boats. Can't hear a hull being planed from here. I'll get used to it. Probably. [#face=sad]
shipwright: I'll fix that loose step of yours at the inn. Not today. Hands still shake. [#face=neutral]
- I'll help you fix it.
	shipwright: You hold, I hammer. When I can hold a hammer. [#face=happy]
- No rush.
	shipwright: There's always a rush. The sea doesn't wait. But thank you. [#face=happy]
=> END

~ home_happy

% shipwright: Heard a keel going down at the shed this morning. Best sound in the world. [#face=happy]
% shipwright: Held a chisel for a whole minute today. Didn't drop it. [#face=tease]
% shipwright: Good house. Good neighbors. Smells right. [#face=happy]
% shipwright: If you see the cat, tell her the Ōkujira says hello. [#face=tease]
=> END

~ home_unhappy

% shipwright: House is fine. Too quiet. I'm used to mallets. [#face=neutral]
% shipwright: Put me by the boat shed or the net shed and I'd sleep through the night. [#face=neutral]
% shipwright: I walk to the boats every morning anyway. Might as well live there. [#face=tease]
=> END

~ no_home

% shipwright: Dock's fine. I've slept under more hulls than roofs. [#face=neutral]
% shipwright: When you've a spare plank or four, a roof would be nice. [#face=neutral]
=> END

~ scene_invite

shipwright: Come by the shed tomorrow night? [#face=flustered]
shipwright: I built a tub. Took a month with these hands. It needs a second opinion. [#face=flustered]
=> END

~ date

if spot == "hot_spring"
	Genzo sits in the Hot Spring with a little wooden boat bobbing in front of him. He looks like a bear who's been ordered to relax. [#face=tease]
	if dates_done > 0
		shipwright: Back again. Good. The boat's been asking after you. [#face=happy]
	else
		shipwright: Don't laugh. My hands wanted something to do. [#face=tease]
else
	Genzo is wedged into a café chair two sizes too small, holding his teacup in both hands so it won't rattle. [#face=tease]
	if dates_done > 0
		shipwright: Same chair. I've made my peace with it. It hasn't made peace with me. [#face=tease]
	else
		shipwright: Nobody told me the chairs were built for children. [#face=tease]
shipwright: I'm no good at this. Talking. Ask me about boats and I'll go all night. Ask me anything else and you'll get a grunt. [#face=neutral]
- Tell me about boats, then.
	set affection += 1
	shipwright: You'll regret that. [#face=happy]
	shipwright: Cedar for the hull, keyaki for the keel, and you steam the ribs until they bend the way they want to. Never force a plank. It remembers. [#face=happy]
- I like grunts.
	set affection += 1
	shipwright: Hm. [#face=flustered]
	shipwright: That was a good one. You're welcome. [#face=tease]
- What else do you like?
	shipwright: Mornings. Rain on a roof I built. Quiet people. [#face=neutral]
	shipwright: Loud ones, sometimes. Depends who. [#face=flustered]
if spot == "hot_spring"
	The little boat drifts against your arm. He reaches for it, and his hand shakes so hard it bobs away again.
else
	He sets the cup down. It rattles anyway. He glares at it like a bent nail.
shipwright: Twenty years these hands never shook. Now I can't hold a cup of tea. [#face=sad]
- (Take his hand.)
	set affection += 1
	His hand is huge and rough, with pale old scars across the knuckles and the gold mark on the back. It goes still in yours.
	shipwright: Huh. That's better. [#face=flustered]
- They'll come back.
	set affection += 1
	shipwright: You say that like you know. [#face=neutral]
	shipwright: I'll take it anyway. [#face=happy]
- Tea's overrated.
	shipwright: Ha! It is. Don't tell the tea stall. [#face=tease]
shipwright: Made you something. Took four nights. Used to take an hour. [#face=flustered]
He sets a wooden boat in front of you, no longer than your thumb. The hull's a little rough, but the lines are true.
- What's it called?
	shipwright: Hasn't got a name. That's your job. [#face=flustered]
- It's beautiful.
	set affection += 1
	shipwright: It's lopsided. But thank you. [#face=flustered]
if spot == "hot_spring"
	shipwright: It floats. I checked. Twice. [#face=tease]
else
	shipwright: Keep it out of your tea. It'll float, but it'll sulk. [#face=tease]
=> END
